      "message": "Hi Elys,\n\n1-2) This change doesn\u0027t/shouldn\u0027t affect Jami authorization mechanisms.\nA peer connection is always authenticated and authorized during the peer-to-peer TLS handshake, and every channel request is also authorized based on existing rules (eg. a contact part of a conversation is authorized to open a channel for that conversation). Blocking a peer closes all transports with that peer including any pending SIP channel and related owned media transport. PS3 fix makes sure the handover mechanism doesn\u0027t trigger in that case (but even before the PS3 fix, the handover wouldn\u0027t have been successful and would have timed out because the new connection would have been refused).\n\n3) The handover is device-specific, at least for now. In the future, the mechanism might be extended to allow handover from eg. computer to phone, but that\u0027s not in the scope of the current work.\n\n4) not covered by the current patchset.\n\n5) Yes, simultaneous recovery is explicitly anticipated, primarily by keeping ICE renegotiation caller-led.\n\nBoth peers may independently request fresh SIP connections.\nOnly the original caller sends the ICE-restarting re-INVITE. The callee sends an OPTIONS probe asking the caller to initiate it. This avoids crossing recovery offers. See sipcall.cpp:570.\nEach side can adopt a matching incoming channel or follow the peer’s in-dialog request to its new channel.\nIf a recovery offer arrives before channel adoption finishes, its answer-side ICE session is retained and associated with that channel when adoption occurs. See sipcall.cpp:4043.\nCompeting channel arrivals can cause the caller to restart ICE again. Recovery completion requires the negotiated ICE session to match the currently adopted SIP transport.\nThere is a dedicated test for this (handover.cpp:349)\n\nThe limitation: there is no explicit shared election selecting one winning SIP connection when both sides open connections. Coordination relies on channel adoption, probes, and following requests. So this is a supported scenario, but not a guarantee that every competing-channel ordering converges.\n\nEach peer also keeps its own original 20-second recovery deadline; simultaneous recovery does not extend it. The regression test exists, but I haven’t run it because the native build remains blocked.\n\n6) Another Network Change: It replaces the connection attempt within the same recovery episode, rather than restarting the whole recovery. beginCallRecovery(true) marks the call as waiting for another channel and forces a new numbered attempt. Results from older locally requested attempts are rejected. The original 20-second deadline is not reset: a second change at 8 seconds leaves approximately 12 seconds.\n\n7) previous SIP channels are recorded in abandonedTransports_ for that specific call. Attempts to follow the SIP dialog back to that transport are rejected.\nHowever, this is not a blanket rejection of SIP traffic on abandoned channels. For instance, we could receive a new incoming call on that channel.\n\n8) Recovery is bounded by the original 20-second deadline. If the peer returns before the deadline, a subsequent attempt or incoming channel can restore the call, provided SIP and ICE recovery finish in time. After timeout, the daemon fails and removes the call. Later reachability does not resurrect it.\n\n10) This logic is independent from JAMS\n\n11) conference support added by later commits in the PS",
